<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  OWASP Dependency Check Suppression File

  Each suppression must be reviewed and approved before adding.
  Document why the CVE is not applicable to this project.
-->
<suppressions xmlns="https://jeremylong.github.io/DependencyCheck/dependency-suppression.1.3.xsd">

  <!--
    CVE-2021-42574 & CVE-2021-42694: Trojan Source (CVSS 8.3)

    These CVEs describe attacks where malicious Unicode bidirectional control
    characters or homoglyphs hide malicious code from human reviewers while
    compilers/interpreters execute it.

    Why NOT applicable to mermaid-mcp:

    1. STRICT SECURITY MODE: We explicitly configure Mermaid with
       securityLevel: "strict" which encodes all HTML tags in text and
       completely disables click handlers - no JavaScript execution path exists.

    2. DOMPURIFY SANITIZATION: Mermaid uses DOMPurify to sanitize all output,
       stripping dangerous elements (script, iframe, object, embed) and
       event handler attributes (onclick, onerror, onload, etc.).

    3. OUTPUT IS STATIC SVG: This tool renders diagrams to static SVG images.
       Even if LLM-generated input contains Unicode tricks, the sanitized
       output cannot execute code.

    4. ATTACK VECTOR MISMATCH: Trojan Source requires a human code review
       deception -> compilation/execution flow. A diagram renderer has no
       such flow - input is sanitized, output is an image.

    Approved: 2025-12-31
  -->
  <suppress>
    <notes><![CDATA[
      Trojan Source: Bidirectional text attack. Not applicable - this tool
      renders diagrams to SVG with securityLevel:strict, no code execution.
    ]]></notes>
    <cve>CVE-2021-42574</cve>
  </suppress>

  <suppress>
    <notes><![CDATA[
      Trojan Source: Homoglyph attack. Not applicable - same rationale as
      CVE-2021-42574. Output is static SVG, not executable code.
    ]]></notes>
    <cve>CVE-2021-42694</cve>
  </suppress>

  <!--
    CVE-2026-23864: OWASP DC CPE-matching false positive against react

    OWASP Dependency Check matches the npm `react` package against the CPE
    `cpe:2.3:a:facebook:react`, which is shared with unrelated products and
    historically produces false positives. The official npm advisory database
    (npm audit) and GitHub Security Advisories list NO CVE affecting
    `react` >= 0.14.0 as of 2026-05-26 — only ancient pre-0.14 XSS issues.

    Why NOT applicable to mermaid-mcp:

    1. TRANSITIVE DEV-ONLY DEPENDENCY: react is pulled in via
       @mermaid-js/mermaid-cli -> @mermaid-js/mermaid-zenuml -> @zenuml/core
       solely so the ZenUML diagram type can render inside the headless
       browser. We never ship React to end users.

    2. OUTPUT IS STATIC SVG: The server runs Mermaid (incl. ZenUML) in a
       sandboxed puppeteer page, serializes the rendered SVG, and returns
       the SVG string. No React runtime is exposed to clients.

    3. NO LIVE ADVISORY: `npm audit` returns 0 vulnerabilities for the full
       tree on this lockfile. The CVE only fires through OWASP DC's CPE
       matcher, which is the classic source of react FPs.

    Approved: 2026-05-26
  -->
  <suppress>
    <notes><![CDATA[
      False positive from OWASP DC CPE matcher against npm `react`.
      No matching advisory in npm/GHSA databases for react >= 0.14.0.
      React is transitive via @mermaid-js/mermaid-cli; output is static SVG.
    ]]></notes>
    <packageUrl regex="true">^pkg:npm/react@.*$</packageUrl>
    <cve>CVE-2026-23864</cve>
  </suppress>

  <!--
    basic-ftp GHSA-c475-qrg2-pj4r (CVSS 10.0) and extract-zip
    GHSA-7pqw-9j4j-h8q3 / CVE-2026-56876 / GHSA-jmr9-qjv8-65gv (CVSS 8.1-8.6)

    Both are reached only through Puppeteer's browser-download package:
    puppeteer@23 -> @puppeteer/browsers -> extract-zip, and
    @puppeteer/browsers -> proxy-agent -> pac-proxy-agent -> get-uri -> basic-ftp.
    The fixes ship only in puppeteer@25, two major versions away.

    Why NOT reachable in mermaid-mcp:

    1. INSTALL-TIME ONLY: extract-zip unpacks the Chrome archive, and
       basic-ftp backs proxy-agent's ftp:// support, during the browser
       download. The server loads @puppeteer/browsers at launch only to
       resolve the installed browser's path; it never downloads anything at
       runtime. The Docker image skips the download entirely
       (PUPPETEER_SKIP_CHROMIUM_DOWNLOAD) and uses system Chromium.

    2. NO ATTACKER-CONTROLLED INPUT: the download URL and archive come from
       Puppeteer's pinned Chrome-for-Testing release, never from tool input.
       No tool accepts URLs, proxies, archives, or FTP endpoints.

    Tracking the puppeteer@25 upgrade in a follow-up issue.

    Approved: 2026-10-22
  -->
  <suppress>
    <notes><![CDATA[
      basic-ftp ReDoS in Client.list(): reached only via @puppeteer/browsers'
      proxy-agent during browser download. No runtime FTP use; fixed in puppeteer@25.
    ]]></notes>
    <packageUrl regex="true">^pkg:npm/basic-ftp@.*$</packageUrl>
    <vulnerabilityName>GHSA-c475-qrg2-pj4r</vulnerabilityName>
  </suppress>

  <suppress>
    <notes><![CDATA[
      extract-zip archive extraction issues: used only by @puppeteer/browsers
      to unpack the pinned Chrome download at install time. Never handles
      tool input; fixed in puppeteer@25.
    ]]></notes>
    <packageUrl regex="true">^pkg:npm/extract-zip@.*$</packageUrl>
    <vulnerabilityName>GHSA-7pqw-9j4j-h8q3</vulnerabilityName>
    <vulnerabilityName>GHSA-jmr9-qjv8-65gv</vulnerabilityName>
    <cve>CVE-2026-56876</cve>
  </suppress>

  <!--
    CVE-2026-65898: DOMPurify < 3.4.11 allowlist mutation (CVSS 3.1 7.2)

    @zenuml/core's prebuilt dist/zenuml.js bundles its own DOMPurify 3.3.1.
    The npm dompurify package itself is 3.4.17 (patched). No release in
    @zenuml/core 3.x bundles a fixed copy (3.50.1 is the latest), and even the
    newest @mermaid-js/mermaid-zenuml (1.0.1) requires @zenuml/core ^3. A fix
    needs mermaid-cli 12 and zenuml 4.

    Why NOT exploitable in mermaid-mcp:

    1. PRECONDITION NEEDS CODE EXECUTION: the flaw lets a registered
       uponSanitizeAttribute hook permanently mutate the shared allowlist. An
       attacker must already run JavaScript in the page to register a hook.

    2. INPUT IS DIAGRAM TEXT: tool input is Mermaid source, rendered in
       headless Chromium with securityLevel: "strict". No tool input becomes
       script, and no hooks are registered from it.

    Scoped to the bundled file only, so any other DOMPurify copy is still
    checked. Tracked for removal in #171.

    Approved: 2026-10-22
  -->
  <suppress>
    <notes><![CDATA[
      DOMPurify 3.3.1 bundled in @zenuml/core's zenuml.js: exploit requires
      registering a sanitize hook (code execution in the page). Input is
      diagram text under securityLevel strict. No semver-compatible fix.
    ]]></notes>
    <filePath regex="true">.*[\\/]@zenuml[\\/]core[\\/]dist[\\/]zenuml\.js$</filePath>
    <cve>CVE-2026-65898</cve>
  </suppress>

</suppressions>
